PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.2/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a polished, clean set. All 120 levels are unique by line logic, trap rate is 0 everywhere, and each intent matches its board. Most levels have a one-sentence insight, and the stated reads check out against the boards. Strengths: the shared exact-fit and overlap vocabulary, the void and stamp chapters (L37-L48, L49-L60), and the smudge and cracked ideas, which are all introduced alone first. Weaknesses: (1) the Inkwell Woods tally chapter (L13-L24) is repetitive. "The count is met, so the rest is paper" or "only n cells left, so all ink" is reused about ten times, with only a few real twists. (2) Several chapter finales (L12, L24, L36, L48, L60, L72, L96) are no harder than their boss levels, by casual win rate or looks. (3) Some intents are thin, one clue and no insight (L57, L60, L58). (4) Some breathers are only a few points easier than their neighbours (L31, L67, L79, L115). (5) Late-game difficulty is on target. The last-third casual average is about 64%, and no level is both unfair and spiky. Mastery of cracked and smudged is tested more than it is deepened. Overall it is solid and shippable, but it needs a sharper aha in the mid chapters to reach Picross S or Nonogram.com standard. Curve: Pacing is good in the first third. Fill, exact-fit, overlap, a twist (L8), a boss (L6) and a finale (L12) each get their own slot, and ideas are added one at a time. The 12-level chapter template (teach, teach, practise, practise, practise, boss, breather, practise, combine, twist, combine, finale) is clear, and it resets the player's confidence every chapter. Problems: (a) Finales do not scale above bosses. Casual win rates are L6 81 vs L12 82, L18 77 vs L24 79, L30 74 vs L36 75, L90 52 vs L96 56. A Masterwork should be the chapter's peak: add a size step or an extra open-line constraint, and aim for 5-10 points lower casual than the boss. (b) The tally chapter (L13-L24) has too much sameness. Reuse of "met count, rest paper" in L13, L16, L17, L18, L19, L20, L22 and L23 dulls the aha, so cut or merge two or three and add a counting idea that is new, such as a tally against a void or a tally that must be reasoned with exact-fit. (c) Color chapters (L25-L36 and L61-L72) lean on touching-colour gap reasoning for a long run. L28, L29, L33 and L35 all rest on "different colours need no gap." Vary them with one cross-colour-only deduction each. (d) Breathers are barely easier than their neighbours (L31 93, L67 91, L79 92, L115 91 versus neighbours near 70-85). They need to read as a rest: smaller, fewer open decisions, no new clue type. (e) Late difficulty is roughly on target, averaging about 64% casual in L81-L120. The troughs are L82, L83, L90, L102, L108 and L114 (51-57%), which is acceptable as long as there is no guessing, and the following breathers (L91, L103, L115) do relieve them. (f) The mechanic order is fine: voids and stamp then colour-cross, then smudged and cracked, then colour-tally, then combos. L45 and L68 are good bridges. Cracked is first taught at 76% and 69% casual (L85-L86), which is low for teach levels. The L86 teach should be softer.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L19 (6/10): It repeats L14's idea (only n cells left), and casual 92% is no easier than L15's neighbours in feel. It is a re-run, not a rest. → Replace it with a small, almost symmetric board with one tally and no second tally, and make it a true quick win.
L22 (6/10): The twist (one ringed 6 closes, one fills) is just two earlier ideas side by side. It is not a real twist, and the board is small with mixed ink. → Make the twist new: a tally whose run split is ambiguous (the count says 6 but two blocks fit), settled by a column.
L31 (6/10): A bit too dense: 14 looks and 18 lines open, and it sits after the L30 boss, but it is not clearly easier than L29 and L32. → Shrink to 8x8, symmetric balloon, with the sandbag read as the only twist.
L57 (6/10): The intent says little (a stamp must lie inside column 1's 7). Row 7's asymmetric needle is accidental rather than taught. It is a lot of picture for one stamped cell. → Rewrite the clue structure so that the stamp decides the whole compass orientation and add a second line that needs it.
L60 (6/10): Intent covers one column only, and 69% casual is just a bit under the L54 boss (67% is lower). A 15x15 finale should be a combined test but it relies on a single stamp read. → Add a second stamp that must be used on the ring's far side, and make it clearly harder than the L54 boss.
L67 (6/10): A breather at 91% that has 19 looks and a similar picture to the prior chapters; the frog notch and eye read are two small ideas. → Remove the pupil asymmetry and keep it as a plain symmetric frog.
L79 (6/10): A breather with casual 92% sits after the boss, which is right, but the candle has a lot of empty paper and the sparks add clutter. → Remove two sparks and enlarge the candle.
L82 (6/10): Casual 57% vs skilled 93%, a big gap with 14x12 size. It is plausibly a long chain, and the intent is a four-clue list. → Cut to 12x10, and offer one extra anchor row to reduce the cascade.
L83 (6/10): Casual 57% / skilled 88% on a combine, the lowest among its neighbours; the lone tell is a single cell (the fold). → Add a second readable row for the fold.
L86 (6/10): Casual 69% on a teach level, with 18 looks and two rule types. → Make it 7x7 with a simpler mug and a more visible edge.
L102 (6/10): Casual 51% with 45 looks, and the intent stacks three tallies plus cross. → Remove one tally.
L112 (6/10): The aha is a single column, the board has a big empty right side. → Re-draw the horse to fill the canvas.
L115 (6/10): A breather that has a hidden-notch aha, but it is not clearly lighter than L113 or L116; the 'Mina' character is neat. → Make the notch the only open question.
L12 (7/10): Casual 82% vs boss L6 81% and only 27 looks vs 28. The finale does not test mastery harder than the boss. → Make it harder than the boss: add one more open-line decision, or change a clue so the waterline read needs one more crossing. Aim for casual about 75%.
L14 (7/10): Nearly the same flow as L13: the ends become paper, then the rest is ink. The aha is small. → Swap in a board where the ringed count cannot be reached without a crossing clue, so the player has to use the columns.
L15 (7/10): Intent reads as two ideas (row 1 and row 9), which blurs the focus. → Keep only the row 1 tally, then make the row 9 count a plain clue.
L16 (7/10): Very close to L15 and L17: a met count plus the last cell. → Make the owed cell depend on an exact-fit column rather than a lone column 8 argument.
L17 (7/10): The blank-counting angle is good but the board has a lot of mirrored symmetry that lets players guess the copy. → Offset the second ear by one cell so that the tally must be used.
L20 (7/10): Good idea (a shared cell), but the cleaner stated read needs two steps and tidy pine geometry gives players the answer by symmetry. → Break the pine's symmetry slightly (a branch off by one) so the tally has to decide.
L23 (7/10): A long chain (row 8, row 7, row 6, row 12) means players need four steps in a fixed order, and the stem leaves a lot of empty paper. → Widen the leaflets to reduce the empty space, or cut one dependent step.
L28 (7/10): Close to L26 and L29 (a colour change replaces the gap). It is slightly mechanical. → Give it a unique angle, such as the rind's colour gap forcing the top-row reading from column 1 and 12 only.
L29 (7/10): Repeats L25-L28 (touch/no-touch). There is no new deduction. → Add a cross-check where a cell is ruled out because its row has no remaining colour, so it moves toward L31's idea.
L32 (7/10): The idea is clever but the asymmetric husk rests on a single long column argument and has lots of empty paper in the corners. → Tighten the canvas to the corn's bounding box; check the left and right husk each carry one deduction.
L33 (7/10): The deduction is only two cells (row 5 paper), and the painting has empty rows at the bottom. → Raise the board's content so row 8 carries more picture, or use the same trick in two columns.
L35 (7/10): A good aha but the left-right shifting pair is quite specific and the apple lower half is plain rows. → Add a distinct third apple gap on another row so the idea repeats once.

